{"type": "step", "node_id": 23, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\nThe sequence $S_n$ is the Lucas sequence $U_n(P,Q)$ with $P=1, Q=-5$, satisfying $S_n = S_{n-1} + 5S_{n-2}$. Its discriminant is $D = P^2 - 4Q = 1+20 = 21$.\n\n**Theory of Lucas sequences.** For a prime $p \\nmid 2QD$, the rank of apparition $\\omega(p)$ (the least $n$ with $p \\mid S_n$) divides $p - \\left(\\frac{D}{p}\\right)$, where $\\left(\\frac{D}{p}\\right)$ is the Legendre symbol. Consequently:\n- If $\\left(\\frac{D}{p}\\right)=1$, then $\\omega(p) \\mid p-1$, so $p \\mid S_{p-1}$.\n- If $\\left(\\frac{D}{p}\\right)=-1$, then $\\omega(p) \\mid p+1$. For $p \\mid S_{p-1}$ we'd need $\\omega(p) \\mid \\gcd(p-1,p+1)=2$, i.e. $\\omega(p)\\in\\{1,2\\}$. But $S_1=S_2=1$, so no prime $>1$ divides them. Thus $p \\nmid S_{p-1}$.\n\nTherefore, for odd primes $p \\notin\\{3,5,7\\}$:\n$$p \\mid S_{p-1} \\iff \\left(\\tfrac{21}{p}\\right)=1.$$\n\n**Special primes.**\n- $p=2$: $S_1=1$, not divisible by 2. ✗\n- $p=3$: $3\\mid 21$ (special); $S_2=1$. ✗\n- $p=5$: $5\\mid Q$ (special); $S_4=11$. ✗\n- $p=7$: $7\\mid 21$ (special); $S_6=96=7\\cdot13+5$. ✗\n\n**Computing $\\left(\\frac{21}{p}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{p}\\right)\\left(\\frac{7}{p}\\right)$ for primes $p\\geq 11$:**\n\nUsing $\\left(\\frac{3}{p}\\right)=1 \\iff p\\equiv\\pm1\\pmod{12}$ and quadratic reciprocity for $\\left(\\frac{7}{p}\\right)$:\n\n| $p$ | $\\left(\\frac{3}{p}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\frac{7}{p}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\frac{21}{p}\\right)$ |\n|-----|-----|-----|-----|\n| 11 | $-1$ ($11\\equiv11$) | $-1$ | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 13 | $+1$ ($13\\equiv1$) | $-1$ | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 17 | $-1$ ($17\\equiv5$) | $-1$ | $+1$ ✓ |\n| 19 | $+1$ ($19\\equiv7$... $19\\bmod12=7\\Rightarrow-1$) | — | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 23 | $-1$ ($23\\equiv11$) | $+1$ | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 29 | $-1$ ($29\\equiv5$) | $+1$ | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 31 | $-1$ ($31\\equiv7$) | $+1$ | $-1$ ✗ |\n| 37 | $+1$ ($37\\equiv1$) | $+1$ | $+1$ ✓ |\n| 41 | $-1$ ($41\\equiv5$) | $-1$ | $+1$ ✓